drm: sun50i-h713-afbd: tell the firmware the target is the panel, not the source

0117 got the firmware to rebuild its window chain on a geometry change, and it
does -- but it rebuilds onto the wrong target. With a 1280x720 source it put
852x480 through the nodes and out at 1280x720, and the wall showed torn
content. Asked directly, through the firmware's own debug shell (win wm, win
wi), it says what it thinks it is doing:

    m_src_cfg        : [0, 0, 20480, 11520]     /16 = 1280x720
    m_dst_cfg        : [0, 0, 20480, 11520]     /16 = 1280x720
    m_src_active_win : [0, 0,  853,  480]
    m_dst_active_win : [0, 0, 1280,  720]
    node 3           : 852x480 -> 1280x720

The panel has dropped out of the chain entirely. Before the republication those
same fields read 30720x17280, which is 1920x1080 in the same 1/16 units.

The reason is here. This record carries a source half and a target half --
doku/86 4 calls words 2..5 "Quell- und Zielgroesse" -- and this function wrote
the source geometry into both halves, and again into both of the 1/16-pixel
window pairs at 28/30 and 32/34. So the firmware was told, literally, that the
target is 1280x720, and it built exactly that.

Put the panel in the target half. In ring mode that is the display mode the
driver already drives; outside ring mode target stays source, because there the
record describes the plane's own frame and there is no second geometry.

The mapping is not inferred from the disassembly but read back out of the
running firmware: 20480 is 1280*16, and it appeared in m_src_cfg the moment
this driver published w = 1280.

diff -ruN a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c	2026-09-07 21:42:37.109274624 +0200
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c	2026-09-07 21:42:37.115278840 +0200
@@ -288,7 +288,8 @@
  * firmware was not measured against is left at zero.
  */
 static void h713_afbd_build_video_info(u32 *rec, unsigned int w,
-				       unsigned int h, unsigned int stride)
+				       unsigned int h, unsigned int stride,
+				       unsigned int dst_w, unsigned int dst_h)
 {
 	memset(rec, 0, H713_VIDEO_INFO_WORDS * sizeof(*rec));
 
@@ -296,8 +297,8 @@
 	rec[1] = 2;			/* record type: video decoder frame */
 	rec[2] = w;
 	rec[3] = h;
-	rec[4] = w;
-	rec[5] = h;
+	rec[4] = dst_w;
+	rec[5] = dst_h;
 	rec[6] = 0;
 	rec[7] = h;
 	rec[8] = 0;
@@ -311,10 +312,17 @@
 	rec[24] = 0;			/* SDR */
 	rec[25] = 0;			/* no HDR metadata buffers */
 	rec[26] = 0;
-	rec[28] = w * 16;		/* display window, 1/16 pixel */
+	/*
+	 * The window manager reads these four straight into m_src_cfg and
+	 * m_dst_cfg, measured through the firmware's own shell (win wm): with
+	 * w = 1280 they came back as [0, 0, 20480, 11520], which is 1280 and
+	 * 720 in 1/16 pixel. So the first pair is what the firmware is being
+	 * handed as the source and the second is what it takes as the target.
+	 */
+	rec[28] = w * 16;		/* source window, 1/16 pixel */
 	rec[30] = h * 16;
-	rec[32] = w * 16;
-	rec[34] = h * 16;
+	rec[32] = dst_w * 16;		/* target window, 1/16 pixel */
+	rec[34] = dst_h * 16;
 }
 
 /*
@@ -423,7 +431,9 @@
 	unsigned int i;
 
 	new_ptr = readl(h->regs + AFBD_VIDEO_INFO0) != ptr;
-	h713_afbd_build_video_info(rec, w, hgt, stride);
+	h713_afbd_build_video_info(rec, w, hgt, stride,
+				   ring ? h->mode.hdisplay : w,
+				   ring ? h->mode.vdisplay : hgt);
 	differs = memcmp(h->video_info, rec, sizeof(rec)) != 0;
 	write_record = differs && (!h->video_info_published || ring);
 	if (differs && h->video_info_published && !ring)
